/* The guided exchange, assembled: the drawer, the transcript, the composer
   that changes shape because the machine offers choices at it, and the rail.

   It runs a script keyed by step index — whatever is typed, pasted or
   improvised at step n produces turn n — so a live demo cannot be broken by a
   typo, a half-paste or a visitor who ignores every option and writes a
   paragraph. The scripts are content and live in `scripts.jsx`; nothing about
   any scenario is written into this component, which is why the same skeleton
   holds a nought-turn errand and a five-turn one without changing shape.

   No step count anywhere, and nothing standing above the transcript either:
   the top carries identity and the way out, and that is all.

   Three regions, three jobs. The transcript records what was said, and nothing
   else: no cards, no lists, no pages. The composer holds what can be said
   next, options included. The rail holds everything the conversation has
   turned up — every relevant page and every person, the recommended one
   standing open with their direct line and the rest folded — in chapters bound
   to the turns that produced them, with the firm's own switchboard permanently
   above all of it.

   Chapters and reading position. Every machine turn carries its chapter index,
   the surface reports which one is at the reading line, and the rail follows:
   scrolling back brings that turn's links and people back. `railMode` decides
   how — `swap` shows the chapter being read alone, `expand` shows all of them
   with the read one emphasised. Getting back to the newest state is one move
   from either end, the transcript's chevron or the rail's own line.

   A refusal is a first-class turn: it answers nothing, offers nothing, and
   leaves the standing question and the convergence indicator exactly as they
   were. Nothing in the interface says a script is running — no counter, no
   badge, no controls. Resetting is reloading. */

